**ZERO verified public benchmark numbers — or any verified public facts at all — were found for this exact model/ID.** Per the self-exclusion rule this file is saved as `GLM_5.3.md.excluded` — no scored `.md` is written, and no placeholder scores are invented.

What was checked (2026-10-02):

- **Identity:** the only trace is this repo's scaffolded stub `meta.json` (`opencode/mai-experimental-test`, `scaffolded: true`, placeholder "128K / text in/out / standard pricing"). The name suggests a Microsoft MAI-family experimental/test SKU (BenchLM tracks MAI-Thinking-1, MAI-Code-1.1-Flash, MAI-Cyber-1-Flash, MAI-Voice/Transcribe models), but no source ties this exact ID to any of them — no vendor confirmation, no announcement.
- **OpenCode Zen docs** (complete endpoints + pricing tables, fetched 2026-10-02): no `mai-experimental-test` anywhere — not in the model list, not in pricing, not in the deprecated list. The `opencode/` ID currently matches no live Zen model; it most plausibly was a transient test alias.
- **BenchLM** (`mai-experimental-test`): 404; no "Mai Experimental Test" entry in the 783-model directory.
- **LLMReference** (`mai-experimental-test`): 404.
- **Artificial Analysis** (`mai-experimental-test`): 404.

Even the stub's specs (128K, text in/out) are scaffold placeholders with no verified source behind them. With no identity, no specs, and no benchmarks there is nothing an honest Overall score could be computed from. A future re-research should look for (a) a live `mai-experimental-test` ID on Zen or any provider, (b) a Microsoft MAI announcement covering an experimental test SKU, or (c) aggregator coverage — then write a fresh `GLM_5.3.md`.

